<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Montenegro</h1><p class="st-meta">1852 CE – 1910 CE · capital Cetinje</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Secular Montenegrin principality of the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty, which won international recognition of its independence at the Congress of Berlin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1852 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danilo-i-prince-of-montenegro">Danilo Petrović-Njegoš</a>, heir of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-montenegro">Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro</a>, declined to become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and proclaimed himself secular prince with Russian backing. He issued a legal code in 1855, imposed taxes on the clans, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirko-petrovic-njegos">Mirko Petrović</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grahovac">Grahovac</a> in 1858, after which Montenegro's borders were first drawn.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danilo-i-prince-of-montenegro">Danilo</a>'s assassination in 1860, his nephew Nikola ruled for over half a century as a patriarchal autocrat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a>, where foreign legations were built. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> remained a poor land of clans and herders, heavily subsidised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Nikola married his daughters into the royal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> and Russia, and granted a constitution only in 1905.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>War against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1876 to 1878 won recognition of independence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1878, nearly doubling the territory and gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niksic">Nikšić</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-montenegro">Bar</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>. On his jubilee in 1910 Nikola proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-montenegro">Kingdom of Montenegro</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-montenegro.jpg" alt="Principality of Montenegro" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: B1mbo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montenegro_(1852%E2%80%931860).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1852 CE – 1910 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cetinje</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Danilo I, Nikola I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Knjaževina Crna Gora</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1881&amp;focus=principality-of-montenegro" aria-label="Principality of Montenegro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5139 670 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Montenegro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5459 926L5459 926M5459 926L5459 926"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5459 926h0M5459 926h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5459 926h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5459 926h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cetinje. <a href="/atlas?year=1881&amp;focus=principality-of-montenegro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
